#!/usr/bin/env bash
# codex-scan — periodic FULL-PASS security + usability audit of an R2 repo, run by
# OpenAI Codex (a different model than the fleet's Claude). This is the recurring,
# institutionalised version of the external hackability probe: a fresh independent
# adversary re-scanning the codebase "now and then" — run it when Codex token usage
# has RESET (a full scan is quota-heavy). Read-only: Codex reads + reports, never edits.
#
# Findings still get TRIAGED against current bytes before any fix (an outside doc can
# be partly stale/wrong) — same discipline as the 2026-06-25 probe.
#
# Usage:
#   codex-scan <repo-dir> [security|usability|both]   # default: both
#   codex-scan <repo-dir> both > findings.md
#
# Env: FLEET_CODEX_BIN (default codex), CODEX_SCAN_TIMEOUT (default 1800s).
set -uo pipefail

CODEX="${FLEET_CODEX_BIN:-codex}"
command -v "$CODEX" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "codex-scan: '$CODEX' not on PATH" >&2; exit 127; }

repo="${1:?usage: codex-scan <repo-dir> [security|usability|both]}"
focus="${2:-both}"
[[ -d "$repo" ]] || { echo "codex-scan: '$repo' is not a directory" >&2; exit 2; }

read -r -d '' SEC <<'EOF'
SECURITY: trust/identity/keys, the wire + dataplane ordering (auth-before-record, dedup, replay),
routing (source-hop, TTL, flood amplification), OTA (signed vs unsigned, recovery atomicity),
provisioning/join (owner-binding, join-code, collision), CBOR canonicalisation, platform receivers
vs the verified core, and any place a lower-level API accepts behaviour a higher gate only sometimes
compensates for. Reproduce the worst finding through the public API if you can.
EOF
read -r -d '' USA <<'EOF'
USABILITY: friction in the happy path (auth/connectivity that should be invisible), one-command-per-intent,
hardcoded credentials / sudo setup / multi-tool sprawl, resumability of dropped steps, calm-by-default with
escalation only at the right moments (key-bearing / ambiguous / ownership-crossing), and whether the operator
ever has to understand crypto/network internals they shouldn't.
EOF

read -r -d '' MISSION <<'EOF'
THE MISSION + VALUES THIS SYSTEM SERVES (audit AGAINST these, not generic best-practice):
- CORE MISSION: design systems that BENEFIT THE PLANET AND ITS INHABITANTS, where distributed AI and the
  natural world are a SYMBIOSIS — mutual benefit, NOT extraction — and that give people PURPOSE (agency,
  kaitiakitanga/guardianship, meaningful participation). AI gains senses; the natural world gains sovereignty,
  voice, protection, and a share of value; people gain meaning and a role.
- DIGITAL SOVEREIGNTY FOR NATURAL ENTITIES: rivers, mountains, ecosystems, communities, and PEOPLE (a person
  wearing devices is a natural entity) are first-class sovereign participants. The senses/data ABOUT an entity
  belong to ITS trust group, stewarded by its kaitiaki (self or designated guardian), consent-governed — NOT
  to whoever holds the sensor or the platform/manufacturer. The keystore/wallet = personal digital
  self-sovereignty, the inverse of surveillance capitalism.
- THE HOW: off- AND on-grid distributed intelligence + senses, through R2. Off-grid reliability
  (partition-tolerant, store-carry-forward, transient networking) is the hard, essential part — it must deliver
  intelligence + senses where there is NO grid (EarthGrids, disaster zones, underserved communities).
- STANDING VALUES / tie-breakers: security WINS conflicts (prefer more secure over more convenient); calmer
  technology in UX (calm-by-default, escalate only when genuinely needed); simplicity (Occam — complexity is a
  liability); confidence is earned by surviving refutation, never by assertion.
EOF

read -r -d '' SOV <<'EOF'
MISSION / SOVEREIGNTY: where is sovereignty merely DESCRIBED but not ENFORCED at runtime (no egress gate /
consent record / provenance / revocation), so senses-about-an-entity could leak or be extracted against the
entity's trust group? Where does the design tend toward extraction, central custody, surveillance, or
lock-in rather than symbiosis + local ownership? Where does off-grid operation silently degrade or depend on
infrastructure (a single gateway/broker) it claims not to need? Where is the person/entity unable to know or
control what data leaves, or to leave? Flag mission-divergence with the same severity rigour as a security bug.
EOF

case "$focus" in
  security) lens="$SEC" ;;
  usability) lens="$USA" ;;
  sovereignty|mission) lens="$SOV" ;;
  both|all|*) lens="$SEC"$'\n\n'"$USA"$'\n\n'"$SOV" ;;
esac

PROMPT="You are an INDEPENDENT adversarial auditor (a different model from the team that wrote this code). \
Do a FULL-PASS audit of this repository for the lenses below, JUDGED IN SERVICE OF THE MISSION + VALUES stated \
first. Default to skepticism — find real problems, do not rubber-stamp. Read the code; cite concrete evidence \
as file:line. For each finding give: an id, severity (critical/high/medium/low), the evidence (file:line), why \
it matters (the concrete attack, friction, or mission-divergence), and a one-line fix. Group by lens. Be honest \
about what is GOOD too (so the team can credit it). Prefer a shorter list of REAL, verified findings over a long \
speculative one.

$MISSION

LENSES:
$lens

Output a structured findings list (markdown). This will be triaged against current code before any fix, \
so cite exact locations."

cd "$repo" || exit 2
echo "# codex-scan: $repo ($focus)"
echo "<!-- run when Codex quota is fresh; triage findings vs current bytes before fixing -->"
echo
exec timeout "${CODEX_SCAN_TIMEOUT:-1800}" "$CODEX" exec \
  --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check "$PROMPT" < /dev/null
